From 004c4eeb961b0cd7587d75b2ac6efad0f1d6003a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 08:01:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GUI: a card view, the risk statement on screen, and two
 working papers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The grid answers how far a subject reaches across the abstraction spine. It
answers badly what one requirement actually says, because a cell has room for
an identifier and two clamped lines. So there is a second view rather than a
compromise between them, and which one you are in is in the URL.

wacc/render/highlight.py marks the words a control was returned for. Two
strengths: what the reader typed, and what a concept added on their behalf.
'patch applications' expands to twenty-five stems including 'system' and
'security', and marking those at full strength marks most of every control on
the page. Escaping happens per span at token boundaries, so the marks are the
only unescaped characters the module emits.

The card carries the risk statement rather than hiding it behind a click.
Communicating what an absent control exposes is what the tool is for. The test
procedure, the links and any CIS product detail load when the reader opens
them, from a fragment route on this same server — HTML rather than JSON so the
escaping and the wording stay in Python where the suite can read them.

Two working papers that did not exist: a risk summary for an executive, which
separates frameworks that were asked and are silent from frameworks nobody
asked, and a test plan with a sign-off row per control, because a test whose
result nobody recorded was not carried out.

An empty query now answers with what is loaded, at what fidelity and under what
licence, instead of an empty grid.

Card columns are measured at the same three widths and recorded beside the
grid's. They fit fewer frameworks, which is the trade the view exists to make.

Four cases the matrix showed were watching nothing:

- The grid's escaping case could not fail. Body text now escapes through
  mark() rather than esc(), so esc() could be removed entirely and the case
  still passed. It checks the identifier now as well as the text.
- The hostile panel case never saw hostile text. _quote() resolves through
  relations.quotable_text(uid) and the hostile control shares a uid with a real
  one, so the corpus text came back instead.
- 'says nothing on this subject' appears once per framework in the coverage
  strip, so looking for it anywhere on the page passed when no card column said
  it. It reads the card's own wording now.
- The highlighter's escaping case used text with no matched word in it, which
  exercises the trailing escape and neither of the other two.

Eleven matrix entries added, two retargeted. 419 checks, 56 of 56 defects
caught, no stale entries.

